WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Two genes steer one cell fate, but which acts first?

2
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:10.060
In hermaphrodite Caenorhabditis elegans, development makes 1,090 somatic cells.

3
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.780
The same 131 die every time, leaving 959.

4
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:22.280
Reproducible death alone does not prove genes cause it, so geneticists test mutants.

5
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:30.090
Start with loss-of-function mutants, where a gene's activity is reduced or

6
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.550
abolished. A null mutant has none left.

7
00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.290
Losing ced-9 removes a brake on death, so killing follows.

8
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.070
Losing ced-3 removes the killer, so doomed cells live.

9
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.730
Single mutants name the parts, not their order.

10
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:53.470
To order them, geneticists put both mutations into one worm, making a double mutant.

11
00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.910
Suppose ced-9 sits upstream as a brake holding back ced-3.

12
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:05.490
In that order, ced-3 acts downstream, carrying out the death that ced-9 normally

13
00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:06.490
prevents.

14
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:11.903
Now remove both genes in the same worm, breaking the brake and the killer together.

15
00:01:13.243 --> 00:01:16.083
Predict what happens to the cells in this double mutant.

16
00:01:17.423 --> 00:01:21.743
Do they die because the brake is gone, or live because the killer is gone too?

17
00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:32.517
The double mutant lives and keeps the cells that normally die, matching the ced-3

18
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:38.537
mutant. Removing the killer cancels the effect of removing the brake, so the brake

19
00:01:38.537 --> 00:01:39.537
works through the killer.

20
00:01:40.677 --> 00:01:46.057
That is epistasis: when two losses have opposite effects, the double mutant usually

21
00:01:46.057 --> 00:01:47.917
shows the later gene's effect.

22
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.060
Two limits keep epistasis honest.

23
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.500
First, the rule expects a linear pathway and opposite loss-of-function mutations.

24
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.400
Experimenters use strong loss-of-function or null mutants, because leftover activity

25
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.220
can blur which phenotype wins.

26
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:12.540
Comparing the double mutant to both single mutants tests which phenotype truly wins.

27
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:17.253
Second, genetic order does not show which proteins touch.

28
00:02:18.573 --> 00:02:24.193
The textbook cartoon draws CED-9 holding CED-4, a partner that switches on CED-3.

29
00:02:25.373 --> 00:02:28.833
The phenotypes stand, yet the mechanism stays less settled.

30
00:02:30.053 --> 00:02:35.073
A 2024 study tested CED-9 mutants that cannot bind CED-4.

31
00:02:35.073 --> 00:02:38.493
They still protected cells in those assays.

32
00:02:39.567 --> 00:02:43.347
Within those limits, epistasis unlocked landmark pathways.

33
00:02:44.827 --> 00:02:51.247
In 1986, Hilary Ellis and Horvitz reported two cell-death genes, ced-3 and ced-4.

34
00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:56.807
Robert Horvitz's laboratory combined mutations to order four genes.

35
00:02:58.247 --> 00:03:02.107
EGL-1 blocks CED-9, and CED-9 holds back CED-4.

36
00:03:03.327 --> 00:03:05.727
In turn, CED-4 switches on CED -3.

37
00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:06.727
In turn, CED -4 switches on CED -4.

38
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.670
Double mutants also ordered yeast secretion.

39
00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:17.610
In 1980, Novick, Field and Schekman found 188 clones that trapped the enzyme

40
00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.930
invertase at 37 °C.

41
00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.410
They sorted them into 23 groups.

42
00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:30.250
Their SEC numbers carried no order, so 1981 double mutants ordered those handoffs.

43
00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:36.210
Combine two mutations in one pathway, and the winning outcome maps their order.
